! EXIC (EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT): the subject is reached through a
! PSEUDO-INSTRUCTION COUNTER, and THE COUNTER IS STEPPED BY THE
! SUBJECT'S LENGTH between the fetch and the execution.
!
! THE RULE, Reference Manual 22-6530-2 printed p.50 (PDF 53 -- PDF page
! = printed page + 3), read first-hand at 300 dpi for this program:
!
!   "Execute Indirect and Count (EXIC).  Bits 0-18 of the full-word
!   addressed by bits 0-17 of the effective address are used as a
!   pseudo-instruction counter.  The contents of the pseudo-instruction
!   counter address the location of a subject instruction that is
!   fetched and executed.  After the subject instruction has been
!   fetched but prior to its execution, the pseudo-instruction counter
!   is stepped.  If the subject instruction is half-length, the
!   pseudo-instruction counter is stepped once; if full-length, twice.
!   When EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT is a subject instruction in a
!   multi-leveled execution operation, each pseudo-instruction counter
!   involved is appropriately stepped between the fetching and
!   executing of its corresponding subject instruction."
!
! and printed p.49, the control rule EXIC shares with EX:
!
!   "When an execution operation is initiated in the main program, the
!   instruction counter is stepped in normal fashion as in all
!   operations of the main program.  However, it is never stepped
!   during the execution of any subject instruction even if the subject
!   instruction is again one of the execution instructions."
!
! THE DECK ORACLE IS jb_ax5b ROUTINE 1262 TEST 2B, 023705.00-024000.00,
! transcribed for its ENCODING as programs/ax5b_162_test2b_head_tail.
! strap and ax5b_162_exic_classes.strap (it cannot be run: it needs
! SSIP, the 64-word interrupt table and routine 1260's subroutines).
! It sweeps five instruction classes with THREE rules each -- the EXIC
! must not interrupt; the subject must have executed; and the pseudo-IC
! must have been stepped by the subject's length -- and it makes the
! manual's "once / twice" executable: its four half-length classes
! check "V+I,$X2,0.32" and its FULL WD VFL class checks "V+I,$X2,1.0".
! THIS FILE USES THE DECK'S OWN IDIOM FOR RULE (c), LV / V+I / KV /
! BZXE, so that a step of the wrong size halts on the row that names it.
!
! THE PSEUDO-INSTRUCTION COUNTERS HERE ARE BUILT BY THE ASSEMBLER, not
! planted at run time as the deck plants them, and that is deliberate.
! Each is a WORD MADE OF TWO BRANCH TEMPLATES ("PSICA B,SUBJA" followed
! by "BEW,SUBJA"), never executed, whose bits 0-18 are the subject's
! half-word address BY CONSTRUCTION -- so the counter's starting value
! needs no set-up instructions that could themselves be wrong, and its
! bits 19-63 carry a rich non-zero pattern (an index field, two opcodes
! and a second address) that the step MUST NOT DISTURB.  The manual
! gives bits 0-18 to the counter and says nothing about the rest; the
! deck's own I62PSI is written "LX,$X0,0", an index word whose count
! and refill fields live down there.  A model that wrote the whole word
! would pass every check made of bits 0-18 and fail the memory checks
! on PSICA/PSICB/PSICC.
!
! WHAT EACH BLOCK IS FOR:
!
!   A  A HALF-LENGTH subject WITH AN OPERAND OF ITS OWN.  Three reads
!      before anything happens -- the pseudo-IC, the subject word, the
!      subject's operand -- where EX needs two.  Stepped ONCE.  This is
!      TEST 2B's DIR IX class.
!   B  A FULL-LENGTH subject, word-aligned.  Stepped TWICE.  TEST 2B's
!      FULL WD VFL class, and the only one of its five that checks
!      "V+I,$X2,1.0".
!   C  A FULL-LENGTH subject at an ODD HALF-WORD, which straddles two
!      storage words and cannot be decoded -- and therefore cannot have
!      its LENGTH read -- until a second word has been fetched.  Also
!      stepped twice.  B and C differ only in the fetch path, so
!      neither alone tests the interaction with the step.
!   D  TWO LEVELS, and the two counters step by DIFFERENT AMOUNTS.  The
!      outer EXIC's subject is an EXIC, which is HALF-length, so the
!      outer counter steps .32; the inner EXIC's subject is FULL, so the
!      inner counter steps 1.0.  This is p.50's "each pseudo-instruction
!      counter involved is appropriately stepped".  A model that sized
!      every step by the FINAL instruction's length gets the outer one
!      wrong; a model that always stepped .32 gets the inner one wrong.
!   E  BITS 0-17, NOT 0-18.  "EXIC,PSICE+.32" must do exactly what
!      "EXIC,PSICE" does, because the manual addresses the counter with
!      bits 0-17 of the effective address -- one bit narrower than EX's
!      bits 0-18.  This is the only block that separates the arm's
!      baWord(effAddr24(...)) from EX's effAddr19.
!      IT PINS THE HARDWARE RULE AND NOT THE ASSEMBLER'S.  STRAP emits
!      bit 18 = 1 here; whether STRAP-1 did, or diagnosed it, is a
!      separate and still-open question (docs/NEXT.md 5d records the
!      same latent gap for LX/SX and deliberately leaves it open).  What
!      this block asserts is only what p.50 states: the MACHINE ignores
!      that bit.
!   F  WHY IT IS CALLED A COUNTER.  Three EXICs through ONE pseudo-IC
!      run three DIFFERENT subjects in sequence -- half, full (and
!      straddling), half -- because each one leaves the counter on the
!      next.  p.50's own programming note: "execution operations in a
!      subroutine may execute parameter-specifying subject instructions
!      whose locations follow that of the branch to the subroutine."
!      A step that is off by one in either direction runs the wrong
!      instruction, not merely the right one with a wrong counter.
!   G  THE PSEUDO-IC IN AN INDEX REGISTER.  p.50: "Pseudo-instruction
!      counters may occupy any of the index registers as well as main
!      core storage locations."  Word 21 is $X5, and the step is an
!      ordinary write to it.
!
! EVERY SUBJECT OUTSIDE BLOCK F IS FOLLOWED BY A "B,$", and those rows
! are the instrument, not padding: if the model returned control to the
! half-word after the SUBJECT rather than after the EXIC, the program
! would halt on one of them.  That is routine 1262 TEST 2A's whole
! subject -- "EXIC MUST NOT RELINQUISH CONTROL".
!
! RUNS DISABLED THROUGHOUT.  Nothing here is about the interruption
! system or address monitoring; programs/exic_addr.strap is, and it
! carries p.32's Data Store rule for the EXIC's own effective address.
! Running disabled keeps protViolates (p.32's enable gate) out of the
! way, so a failure here can only be about the execution operation.
! The closing "BAD,$" is TEST 2B's rule (a) in the form this model can
! state it: nothing in this program may raise an address alarm.
!
! TEN INJECTIONS MOVE THIS PROGRAM, EACH HALT OBSERVED (not predicted).
! Every one was applied to a pristine snapshot of bsv/StretchExecute.bsv
! by an exact-substring substitution that ABORTS unless its anchor occurs
! exactly once, and printed its unified diff before the run -- because a
! `sed` that silently fails to match prints ALL TESTS PASSED, which reads
! exactly like an honest "this does not discriminate".  After each run
! the patch was REVERSED and the file compared byte-for-byte with the
! snapshot.  Reproduce any row with
! `python3 line_addresses.py programs/exic_subject.strap`.
!
!   step size always ONE                   -> 6159  006007.40
!     (`HWordAddr step = 1;`)                    BZXE, $       (B)
!   step size always TWO                   -> 6151  006003.40
!     (`HWordAddr step = 2;`)                    BZXE, $       (A)
!   the step CLOBBERS bits 19-63           -> 6151  006003.40
!     (`{ c.sa + step, 45'b0 }`)                 BZXE, $       (A)
!   no step at all (the writeWord           -> 6151  006003.40
!     replaced by noAction)                      BZXE, $       (A)
!   the subject installed under its OWN    -> 6401  006200.40
!     pc and len (`rpc = c.sa; rlen =            WRONGA B, $
!     slen;` before the step's defer)            -- control relinquished
!   bit 18 of the effective address        -> 6186  006025.00
!     allowed to reach the counter's             BZXE, $       (E)
!     WORD address
!   the straddling subject's second read   -> 6164  006012.00
!     dropped (`if (False)` in place of          B, $          (C)
!     the EXIC straddle test)
!   the COUNTER WORD executed instead of   -> 6151  006003.40
!     read (`sa: {pw, 1'b0}`)                    BZXE, $       (A)
!   the subject not marked `under`         -> 6449  006230.40
!     (`under: False` in shape 2's rex)          WRONGI B, $
!                                                -- the branch was not
!                                                   suppressed
!   a level whose subject is ITSELF an     -> 6176  006020.00
!     execution operation does not step          BZXE, $       (D)
!     its counter -- a plausible                 -- and PSICD2, the INNER
!     misreading of "between the fetching           counter, stepped
!     and executing of its corresponding            correctly, which is
!     subject instruction"                          what makes the row
!                                                   name the OUTER one
!
! BLOCK D IS PROVEN BY THAT LAST ROW AND BY NOTHING ELSE, which is why
! it was built: every other injection that touches the step halts in
! block A or block B, upstream of D.  D is the only block in either
! program whose subject is an execution operation, and therefore the
! only one that separates "each counter stepped by ITS OWN subject's
! length" from "every counter stepped by the final instruction's".
!
! WHICH CHECK NO ROW REACHES, said rather than left to be found.  Block
! H's SUBJH memory check -- "the counter word was executed, so the word
! holding the subject was zeroed" -- is belt-and-braces behind block A:
! the same injection makes block A's counter check fail three rows in,
! and that is where the program halts.  Block H's independent value is
! that it states the contract (1262 TEST 2A's "-INST AT PSIC WAS
! EXECUTED.") and would name it if blocks A-G were removed.
!
! SIX FURTHER INJECTIONS LEAVE THIS PROGRAM GREEN, and that is correct
! rather than a gap: the counter's low-address guard, the subject's
! low-address guard and the Data Store rule are exercised by
! programs/exic_addr.strap, which halts on each.
!
! Instructions used: BD, Z, LV, V+I, KV, LVI, LX, SX, EXIC, BZXE, BB,
! BAD, B, BEW, L(BU) -- all implemented.

        SLC, (8)6000.0
START   BD,  $+.32                 ! disabled: see the header
!
! ---- A. a HALF-LENGTH subject with an operand of its own ----------
        Z,   $X1
        LV,  $X2, PSICA            ! the counter BEFORE
        EXIC, PSICA                ! subject "LX,$X1,SRCA", half-length
        SX,  $X1, RESA             ! reached only if control came back HERE
        V+I, $X2, 0.32             ! p.50: half-length -> stepped ONCE
        KV,  $X2, PSICA
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter is not BEFORE + .32
!
! ---- B. a FULL-LENGTH subject, word-aligned ----------------------
        Z,   $R
        LV,  $X2, PSICB
        EXIC, PSICB                ! subject "L(BU,64,8),SRCB", full word
        LX,  $X3, $R
        SX,  $X3, RESB
        V+I, $X2, 1.0              ! p.50: full-length -> stepped TWICE
        KV,  $X2, PSICB
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter is not BEFORE + 1.0
!
! ---- C. a FULL-LENGTH subject that straddles two storage words ----
!   "BB1,MARKC.0,WRONGC": branch if bit 0 is ONE, and set bit 0 to one
!   either way.  MARKC's bit 0 starts at ZERO, so the branch condition
!   is NOT met -- no branch and (p.49) no EXE, because EXE is turned on
!   only "by any branch operation of a subject instruction in which the
!   condition for a successful branch is met".  The bit change is
!   therefore an ordinary unsuppressed one, and it is what says the
!   straddling subject was decoded at all: a model that took the second
!   half-word from the wrong storage word would decode something else.
        LV,  $X2, PSICC
        EXIC, PSICC
        BB,  MARKC.0, C1           ! bit 0 must now be ONE
        B,   $                     ! hang: the straddling subject did not run
C1      V+I, $X2, 1.0              ! ... and it was still FULL-length
        KV,  $X2, PSICC
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter is not BEFORE + 1.0
!
! ---- D. two levels, two counters, two DIFFERENT step sizes --------
        Z,   $R
        LV,  $X2, PSICD1           ! the OUTER counter
        LV,  $X3, PSICD2           ! the INNER counter
        EXIC, PSICD1               ! subject "EXIC,PSICD2" -- HALF-length
        LX,  $X4, $R               ! ... whose subject is a FULL word
        SX,  $X4, RESD
        V+I, $X2, 0.32             ! the outer subject was an EXIC: HALF
        KV,  $X2, PSICD1
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: outer counter stepped wrongly
        V+I, $X3, 1.0              ! the inner subject was a VFL full word
        KV,  $X3, PSICD2
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: inner counter stepped wrongly
!
! ---- E. bits 0-17 of the effective address, not bits 0-18 ---------
        Z,   $X6
        LV,  $X2, PSICE
        EXIC, PSICE+.32            ! bit 18 SET, and the machine ignores it
        SX,  $X6, RESE
        V+I, $X2, 0.32
        KV,  $X2, PSICE
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: bit 18 reached the counter's address
!
! ---- F. three EXICs through ONE counter, in sequence --------------
        Z,   $X7
        Z,   $X8
        Z,   $R
        EXIC, PSICF                ! -> SUBJF1 "LX,$X7,SRCA", half
        EXIC, PSICF                ! -> SUBJF2 "L(BU,64,8),SRCB", full,
                                   !    and it straddles (it begins at .40)
        EXIC, PSICF                ! -> SUBJF3 "LX,$X8,SRCC", half
        SX,  $X7, RESF1
        LX,  $X9, $R
        SX,  $X9, RESF2
        SX,  $X8, RESF3
!
! ---- G. the pseudo-instruction counter IN AN INDEX REGISTER -------
        Z,   $X11
        LVI, $X5, SUBJG            ! $X5's value field addresses SUBJG
        EXIC, $X5                  ! word 21 IS the counter
        SX,  $X11, RESG
        LVI, $X12, SUBJG+.32       ! where the step must have left it
        KV,  $X12, $X5
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the register counter did not step
!
!
! ---- H. THE COUNTER WORD IS DATA, AND IS NEVER EXECUTED -----------
!   PSICH is itself a REAL INSTRUCTION -- "Z,SUBJH", a store-zero whose
!   address field IS the subject's location, so its bits 0-18 are the
!   counter and its opcode is live.  The deck's own counter is the same
!   shape: PDF p.109 prints "I62PSI LX,$X0,0  -PSEUDO IC." at 024323.00,
!   a real LOAD INDEX, and routine 1262 TEST 2A zeroes $X0 before the
!   EXIC and checks it after under "-INST AT PSIC WAS EXECUTED."  A
!   model that DECODED the word at the counter's address instead of
!   using its bits 0-18 as an address would run "Z,SUBJH" here and zero
!   the word holding the subject -- which the SUBJH memory check sees
!   directly, and which leaves RESH at zero besides.  (TEST 2B has no
!   such check anywhere; it is TEST 2A that carries this contract.)
        Z,   $X10
        LV,  $X2, PSICH
        EXIC, PSICH
        SX,  $X10, RESH            ! the SUBJECT ran, not the counter word
        V+I, $X2, 0.32
        KV,  $X2, PSICH
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter stepped wrongly
!
! ---- I. the EXE rule reaches an EXIC's subject too, AND THE COUNTER
!         IS STEPPED ANYWAY ---------------------------------------
!   Printed p.49: "The execution exception indicator (EXE) is turned on
!   by any branch operation of a subject instruction in which the
!   condition for a successful branch is met.  When indicator EXE is
!   actuated, the branch and all associated operations are not
!   performed."  The subject here is an unconditional branch, so the
!   condition is met by definition.  This is the one place an EXIC uses
!   the ExSubject context it builds for itself rather than machinery EX
!   already exercises.
!   AND THE COUNTER STEPS EVEN SO, which is a consequence of p.50's
!   own wording rather than a choice: the step happens "after the
!   subject instruction has been FETCHED but prior to its EXECUTION",
!   and an EXE suppression happens during execution.
        BB,  $EXE, $               ! EXE must be OFF to start with
        LV,  $X2, PSICI
        EXIC, PSICI                ! subject "B,WRONGI", condition MET
        BZB, $EXE, $               ! hang: EXE did not come on
        V+I, $X2, 0.32
        KV,  $X2, PSICI
        BZXE, $                    ! hang: the counter did not step
        BEXEZ, $+.32               ! clear EXE before the closing BEW
!
        BAD, $                     ! hang: something raised an address alarm
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the subjects, each with its "control did not come back" trap --
        SLC, (8)6200.0
SUBJA   LX,  $X1, SRCA
WRONGA  B,   $                     ! control returned after the SUBJECT
!
        SLC, (8)6202.0
SUBJB   L(BU,64,8), SRCB           ! a full word at a .00 half-word
WRONGB  B,   $
!
!   Block C's subject: a half-word filler first, so the full word that
!   follows begins at .40 and its two half-words live in two different
!   storage words.
        SLC, (8)6204.0
FILLC   B,   $                     ! never executed; here for its LENGTH
SUBJC   BB1, MARKC.0, WRONGC       ! full word, beginning at 6204.40
WRONGC  B,   $
!
!   Block D's two levels.
        SLC, (8)6210.0
SUBJD1  EXIC, PSICD2               ! HALF-length: the outer counter steps .32
WRONGD1 B,   $
        SLC, (8)6211.0
SUBJD2  L(BU,64,8), SRCD           ! FULL: the inner counter steps 1.0
WRONGD2 B,   $
!
        SLC, (8)6214.0
SUBJE   LX,  $X6, SRCB
WRONGE  B,   $
!
!   Block F's three subjects, CONTIGUOUS and with no traps between
!   them: the counter walks from one to the next, which is the point.
!   SUBJF2 is a full word beginning at .40, so it straddles as well.
        SLC, (8)6220.0
SUBJF1  LX,  $X7, SRCA             ! half  -> counter += .32
SUBJF2  L(BU,64,8), SRCB           ! full  -> counter += 1.0  (straddles)
SUBJF3  LX,  $X8, SRCC             ! half  -> counter += .32
WRONGF  B,   $
!
        SLC, (8)6224.0
SUBJG   LX,  $X11, SRCC
WRONGG  B,   $
!
!   Block H's subject, word-aligned so that "Z,SUBJH" -- the counter
!   word itself -- addresses exactly this word.
        SLC, (8)6226.0
SUBJH   LX,  $X10, SRCD
WRONGH  B,   $
!
        SLC, (8)6230.0
SUBJI   B,   WRONGI                ! an unconditional branch: EXE suppresses it
WRONGI  B,   $                     ! ... so control must NEVER come here
!
! ---- the pseudo-instruction counters ------------------------------
!   Two branch templates per word, never executed.  Bits 0-18 of the
!   LEFT half-word are the counter; everything else is the pattern the
!   step must leave alone.
        SLC, (8)6300.0
PSICA   B,   SUBJA
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6301.0
PSICB   B,   SUBJB
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6302.0
PSICC   B,   SUBJC
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6303.0
PSICD1  B,   SUBJD1
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6304.0
PSICD2  B,   SUBJD2
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6305.0
PSICE   B,   SUBJE
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6306.0
PSICF   B,   SUBJF1
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6307.0
PSICH   Z,   SUBJH                 ! a LIVE instruction: see block H
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
        SLC, (8)6310.0
PSICI   B,   SUBJI
        B,   $                     ! I62BR1: control must never come HERE
!
! ---- data ---------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)6400.0
SRCA    DD(BU,64,8), (8)123456701234
        SLC, (8)6401.0
SRCB    DD(BU,64,8), (8)765432107654
        SLC, (8)6402.0
SRCC    DD(BU,64,8), (8)246713570246
        SLC, (8)6403.0
SRCD    DD(BU,64,8), (8)135724670135
        SLC, (8)6404.0
MARKC   DD(BU,64,8), 0             ! bit 0 starts at ZERO: see block C
        SLC, (8)6405.0
RESA    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6406.0
RESB    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6407.0
RESD    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6410.0
RESE    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6411.0
RESF1   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6412.0
RESF2   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6413.0
RESF3   DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6414.0
RESG    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        SLC, (8)6415.0
RESH    DD(BU,64,8), 0
        END, START
